#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278490 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le e.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{(2x+y)-y}{\\sqrt{6}y}\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{y=1}^{y=e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278491 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=5x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x-y)=5,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+(5x-y)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\sqrt{27}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}(26{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[26{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-5x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(52{x}^{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-20{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{104{x}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}104{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[26{x}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =26\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278492 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+8y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+8y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+8y)=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{z}{36x}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4x+8y}{36x}\\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,9\\frac{4(x+2y)}{36x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(1+\\frac{2y}{x})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[y+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x}-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278493 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}(1+x+y)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}\\,\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-\\frac{1}{1+x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278495 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=3x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 5$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,-3\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=3u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨3u-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3u-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-2v)\\cdot (-3)+(-v)\\cdot (2)+2u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+6v-2v+2u \\\\  & =-7u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-7u+4v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({\\color{blue}-7u+4v})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278506 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xyz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xy(2x+y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{xy(2x+y)}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}-y,\\hspace{0.222em}xyz⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 2-(-y)\\cdot (1)+xy(2x+y))\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+y+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+\\frac{1}{2}+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278507 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278509 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨xy,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=x+y} \\\\  & =⟨xy,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x+y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{xy}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{x+y}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨xy,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{z}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(xy)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot 1+{e}^{x+y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-xy-x-y+{e}^{x+y})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{x+y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+{e}^{2x}-{e}^{x})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-e)-(0+\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2}-8e-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278510 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-2v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u-2v} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v-(u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}3v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}3v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(3v-u)\\cdot 2+(u+v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u+6v-2u+u+v \\\\  & =-2u+7v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2u+7v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\color{blue}(-2u+7v)}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278512 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+({\\color{blue}-Q}\\hspace{0.167em})\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{{\\color{blue}∂z}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278515 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=-{x}^{2}z$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}xz+Q+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $\\mathbf{j}$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=-{x}^{2}z,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}-{x}^{2}z,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2xz,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -2xz & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{x}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-2xz,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf{j}$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}=-{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'}=⟨2xz,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,z))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,R\\hspace{0.222em}⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}2P}\\hspace{0.167em}xz+Q+{\\color{blue}R}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278516 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=2yz$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(P+\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}z+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=2yz,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(y,z)=⟨2yz,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨2z,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =⟨2y,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2z & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2y & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(y,z))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2y⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-2Q})\\hspace{0.167em}z+(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-2R})\\hspace{0.167em}y\\hspace{0.167em})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278518 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+2z,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+2z,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=v-u} \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}v+2(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}3v-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}3v-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(v-u)\\cdot 1+(3v-2u)\\cdot (-1)+(2u-v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u-3v+2u+2u-v \\\\  & =3u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+2z,\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3u-3v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\color{blue}(3u-3v)}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278519 :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(P-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}-R\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{z}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P-{\\color{blue}Q}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\frac{∂k}{∂y}}-R\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\frac{∂k}{∂z}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278522 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{1}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(x+y))+y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}(x+y)+y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}(x+y)+y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278545 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x+{e}^{x}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot ({e}^{x})-(x+{e}^{x})\\cdot 0+0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y{e}^{x})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-y{e}^{x})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}{e}^{x}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{x}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278546 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(xy)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}(xy))=\\frac{y}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy)} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}(xy))=\\frac{x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{tan}(xy)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy)}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy)}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy),\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy))\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy)}\\right)-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy))\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(xy)}\\right)+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(-y-x+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-xy+y]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278547 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2\\sqrt{xy}$ for $3\\le x\\le 4$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{4xy+1}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}+4xy+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy+1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{x}{y}+4xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{[-x\\operatorname{ln}y+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(-x\\operatorname{ln}2+8x+0-2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(6-\\operatorname{ln}2)x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6-\\operatorname{ln}2){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(6-\\operatorname{ln}2)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278548 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}y-x,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}y-x,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}y-x,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x+y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{y-x}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}y-x,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot (2x)-(y-x)\\cdot (2y)+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2}+2xy+1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[y-{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(1-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-(-1+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3})]\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})-(-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278551 :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2yz,\\hspace{0.222em}2x(y+z)⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2yz,\\hspace{0.222em}2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2yz,\\hspace{0.222em}2xz⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\ \\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y(2x+y),\\hspace{0.222em}2x(y+2x+y)⟩{|}_{z=2x+y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{3{x}^{3}y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{4xy+2{y}^{2}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{4xy+4{x}^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3{x}^{3}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2yz,\\hspace{0.222em}2x(y+z)⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3{x}^{3}y)\\cdot (2)-(4xy+2{y}^{2})\\cdot (1)+4xy+4{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6{x}^{3}y-2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}y]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{x}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{2x}{3}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-12-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281298 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{5}{t},\\,t>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0.5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=\\frac{5}{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0.5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $5$ | $2.5$ | $1.67$ | $1.25$ | $1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/97/9707a9d11909ba22a0b90dc3e65aaa0c1bba657cbe42006719b55d4a937573f5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281325 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2}+w,\\,y={u}^{3}+v,\\,z=uv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 2uv & 1 \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281326 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u{e}^{w} \\\\ w-u \\\\ w{e}^{u}-3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u{e}^{w},\\,y(u,v,w)=w-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u}-3v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =u{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-3, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & 0 & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ w{e}^{u} & -3 & {e}^{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3({e}^{w}+u{e}^{w}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{w}(1+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281327 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ u+3w \\\\ -v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,z(u,v,w)=-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6u-0 \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281328 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}(u\\hspace{0.167em}v\\hspace{0.167em}w) \\\\ u+2w \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w),\\,y(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)=uv.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\frac{1}{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} & \\frac{1}{w} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix}+2(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(u-0)-2(1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{u}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281329 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281330 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281331 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=y-3z,\\,v=x+2z,\\,w=3x-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(0-6)-3(-1-0) \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281332 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=3x+yz,\\,v=z,\\,w=-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & z & y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(0+1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281334 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x} \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)={e}^{x},\\,v(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y},\\,w(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & ={e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x+2y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+2y+z} & ={e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z} \\\\  & =uvw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281335 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ z-y \\\\ xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=x,\\,v(x,y,z)=z-y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ y & x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-x) \\\\  & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281336 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=xy,\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y\\begin{vmatrix}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}-x\\begin{vmatrix}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y(xz-0)-x(0-yz) \\\\  & =xyz+xyz \\\\  & =2xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz & =2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281337 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y{z}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2},\\,v(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2},\\,w(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={z}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2xz & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={y}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2xy & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 2xz & 0 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2yz\\begin{vmatrix}2xz & 0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}(0-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+2yz(4{x}^{2}yz-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} & =9{({x}^{3}{y}^{3}{z}^{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9{(y{z}^{2}\\cdot z{x}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9(uvw)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281338 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=uv,\\,y(u,v,w)=u+v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-u=0$ defines the plane $v=u..$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281339 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ {v}^{3}+{w}^{3} \\\\ v+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{3}+{w}^{3},\\,z(u,v,w)=v+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3{w}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3{v}^{2}-3{w}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2}-{w}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}-{w}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting planes $v=w$ and $v=-w.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281340 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{u} \\\\ vw \\\\ u+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\sqrt{u},\\,y(u,v,w)=vw,\\,z(u,v,w)=u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & w & v \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\begin{vmatrix}w & v \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}(w-0)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}} & =0 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w=0$ defines the plane $w=0,$ i.e., $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281341 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2u+v+3w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v{e}^{w},\\,z(u,v,w)=2u+v+3w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3{e}^{w}-v{e}^{w}) \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(3-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{w}(3-v) & =0 \\\\ 3-v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-v=0$ defines the plane $v=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281342 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+3w \\\\ v+2w \\\\ {v}^{2}-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)={v}^{2}-u.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4v)+3(0+1) \\\\  & =3-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3-4v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-4v=0$ defines the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281348 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v{e}^{u} \\\\ u{e}^{v}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v{e}^{u} & {e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{v} & u{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v{e}^{u}\\cdot u{e}^{v}-{e}^{v}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =uv{e}^{u+v}-{e}^{u+v} \\\\  & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,1)} & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v}\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=1} \\\\  & =(-1\\cdot 1-1){e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-2|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =2\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281352 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2v)-(2u)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2v-2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v-2u & <0 \\\\ 2v & <2u \\\\ v & <u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/83/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png)"}]}
